# Verification Levels

Code inspection is not testing.

The agent must not claim that code works merely because it appears correct. Any meaningful code change must be verified by running the relevant command, test, script, build, or application flow.

## Core Rule

The agent must verify work by executing it whenever execution is possible.

Statements like “this looks correct,” “should work,” or “the logic seems sound” are not valid verification.

The agent must provide evidence of verification by reporting:

1. The exact command that was run.
2. Whether it passed or failed.
3. Relevant output, error messages, or observed behavior.
4. Any limitations of the verification.

## Verification Levels

Use the strongest applicable verification level for the task.

### Level 0 — No Verification

Allowed only when execution is impossible in the current environment.

The agent must clearly say that verification was not performed and explain why.

Acceptable:

> I could not run the app because dependencies are not installed in this environment.

Unacceptable:

> This should work.

### Level 1 — Static Inspection

Reading code, checking types mentally, or reviewing files.

This is useful for understanding but does not count as testing.

The agent must not stop at this level if execution is possible.

### Level 2 — Command Execution

Run a relevant command that checks the changed code.

Examples:

```bash
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run build
python -m pytest
ruff check .
go test ./...
cargo test